#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100626 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "79107609-77e6-55e9-970d-da63c39daf0a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of a Curve Defined Parametrically"}] :question/id #uuid "cbd0cb3d-cf0f-54db-8cfc-c938e463e4f8" :question/math-academy-id "e-5087" :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the curve defined parametrically by\n\n$$\nx=2t^2, \\,\\,\\, y=t^2+1, \\quad t \\geq 0.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation, we eliminate $t.$ First we use the $x$ equation to solve for $t^2\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\nx=2t^2 \\quad \\Rightarrow\\quad  t^2 = \\dfrac{x}{2}\n$$\n\nNow substituting $t^2=\\dfrac{x}{2}$ into our expression for $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={t}^{2}+1 \\\\  & =\\frac{x}{2}+1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}x+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the given curve is $y=\\dfrac{1}{2}x+1.$\n\nFinally, since $t \\geq0$ and $x=2t^2,$ we must have $x \\geq 0.$\n\nSo, the complete Cartesian equation is\n\n$$\ny=\\dfrac{1}{2}x+1, \\quad x \\geq 0 .\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100627 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "78dbecb4-68d4-53b0-b47f-f37ce92da896" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of a Curve Defined Parametrically With Trigonometric Functions"}] :question/id #uuid "f3d396ea-caed-51ed-a4d8-0bfc3301f2f3" :question/math-academy-id "e-5101" :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the curve defined parametrically as \n\n$$\nx=\\csc{t},\\,\\,\\, y=2\\cot{t}, \\quad 0 < t < \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's make the trigonometric functions the subject of each equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{csc}⁡t\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{csc}⁡t=x \\\\ y=2\\operatorname{cot}⁡t\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{cot}⁡t=\\frac{y}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDividing the Pythagorean identity $\\sin^{2}{t}+\\cos^{2}{t}=1$ by $\\sin^{2}{t},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡t}{{sin}^{2}⁡t}+\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡t}{{sin}^{2}⁡t} & =\\frac{1}{{sin}^{2}⁡t} \\\\ 1+{(\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t})}^{2} & ={(\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t})}^{2} \\\\ 1+{cot}^{2}⁡t & ={csc}^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\csc{t} = x$ and $\\cot t =\\dfrac{y}{2}$ into this formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1+{(\\frac{y}{2})}^{2} & ={x}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{4} & ={x}^{2}-1 \\\\ {y}^{2} & =4({x}^{2}-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the given curve is $y^2 = 4\\left( x^2-1 \\right).$\n\nFinally, since $0 < t < \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}$ and $x=\\csc t,$ the interval on the $x$-axis is $x \\geq 1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186285355 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "78dbecb4-68d4-53b0-b47f-f37ce92da896" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of a Curve Defined Parametrically With Trigonometric Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "66417d04-d38c-5e39-9e51-1e8879351c79" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(\\frac{x-3}{5})}^{2}+{(\\frac{y+2}{3})}^{2}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285357} {:answer/id #uuid "64d67936-1aa5-5a94-9279-d04853ab073a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(\\frac{x+2}{5})}^{2}+{(\\frac{y-3}{3})}^{2}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285358} {:answer/id #uuid "7f10cc1e-080c-5d30-8843-4dcaa663c37a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(\\frac{x-3}{2})}^{2}+{(\\frac{y+2}{5})}^{2}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285359} {:answer/id #uuid "d59c6013-c23d-583d-a72a-ed6006b07140" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(\\frac{x-5}{3})}^{2}+{(\\frac{y+3}{2})}^{2}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285360} {:answer/id #uuid "e3ac4f4b-b449-54c5-878c-85c1f9ca6735" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(\\frac{x-3}{2})}^{2}+{(\\frac{y+5}{3})}^{2}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285361}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "66417d04-d38c-5e39-9e51-1e8879351c79" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(\\frac{x-3}{5})}^{2}+{(\\frac{y+2}{3})}^{2}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285357} :answer-field/id #uuid "cf047c30-261c-5c45-8660-2be9d0c04098" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285356}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d7c181af-f10d-5053-8fb3-b6d77c28247a" :question/math-academy-id "q-102682" :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the curve defined parametrically as \n\n$$\nx=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+3,\\,\\,\\,y=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-2,\\,t\\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's make the trigonometric functions the subject of each equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+3\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t=\\frac{x-3}{5} \\\\ y=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-2\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t=\\frac{y+2}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the Pythagorean identity ${sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t=1$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\frac{x-3}{5})}^{2}+{(\\frac{y+2}{3})}^{2}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the given curve is ${(\\frac{x-3}{5})}^{2}+{(\\frac{y+2}{3})}^{2}=1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101123 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "79107609-77e6-55e9-970d-da63c39daf0a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of a Curve Defined Parametrically"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "80ac2898-7d60-58c9-b877-aa6227161d83" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = (\\frac{1 - 2x}{x})^{2}, x \\ne 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098509} {:answer/id #uuid "9ac6f5f2-d9b6-5628-9395-de2646e703c4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = (\\frac{1 + 2x}{x})^{2}, x \\ne 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098510} {:answer/id #uuid "d885f9bc-d333-5c26-94ca-f091a0146843" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = (\\frac{-1 - 2x}{x})^{2}, x \\ne 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098511} {:answer/id #uuid "0de38c6c-73ed-505c-840c-7effcf16fed4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = - (\\frac{1 + 2x}{x})^{2}, x \\ne 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098512} {:answer/id #uuid "524dabc5-8fef-57c2-94e3-cb9f7ccf3962" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = - (\\frac{-1 + 2x}{x})^{2}, x \\ne 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098513}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9ac6f5f2-d9b6-5628-9395-de2646e703c4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = (\\frac{1 + 2x}{x})^{2}, x \\ne 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098510} :answer-field/id #uuid "2589f729-9ccd-551b-a6cc-0156d810303c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098508}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5acf88e6-b052-5b29-9849-a312529836f0" :question/math-academy-id "q-15678" :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the curve defined parametrically by\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{1}{t-2},\\,\\,\\,y={t}^{2},\\,t\\ne 2.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation, we eliminate $t.$ First, we use the $x$ equation to solve for $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{1}{t-2} \\\\ x(t-2) & =1 \\\\ t-2 & =\\frac{1}{x} \\\\ t & =\\frac{1}{x}+2 \\\\ t & =\\frac{1+2x}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow substituting $t=\\frac{1+2x}{x}$ into our expression for $y={t}^{2},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={(\\frac{1+2x}{x})}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the given curve is $y={(\\frac{1+2x}{x})}^{2}.$\n\nFinally, since $t\\ne 2$ and $x=\\frac{1}{t-2},$ we must have $x\\ne 0.$\n\nSo, the complete Cartesian equation is\n\n$$\ny={(\\frac{1+2x}{x})}^{2},\\,x\\ne 0.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186285362 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "78dbecb4-68d4-53b0-b47f-f37ce92da896" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of a Curve Defined Parametrically With Trigonometric Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "77164973-aa24-527c-a317-f8d784f5434a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y-3)^{2}+(x+2)^{2}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285364} {:answer/id #uuid "e2ec2db6-cfc3-5c6b-b7b6-67c8d8b29b77" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}+(x+3)^{2}=3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285365} {:answer/id #uuid "da8025c4-36c6-5909-a799-b25ebd05ecc0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y+3)^{2}+(x+2)^{2}=3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285366} {:answer/id #uuid "006f8b63-3556-56db-adbc-896b2ae222b3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y-2)^{2}+(x-3)^{2}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285367} {:answer/id #uuid "ed3965cb-daa0-571c-ad5a-7b1e6b2a3cae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y-3)^{2}+{x}^{2}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285368}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "77164973-aa24-527c-a317-f8d784f5434a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y-3)^{2}+(x+2)^{2}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285364} :answer-field/id #uuid "191f1b4c-768e-5f06-b2e7-4d5b537e182d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285363}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e929f172-a4de-58f2-bf84-26e1ce65ea1f" :question/math-academy-id "q-15679" :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the curve defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-2,\\,\\,\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+3,\\,t\\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's make the trigonometric functions the subject of each equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-2\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t=x+2 \\\\ y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+3\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t=y-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the Pythagorean identity ${sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n(y-3)^{2}+(x+2)^{2}=1.\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the given curve is $(y-3)^{2}+(x+2)^{2}=1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101124 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "78dbecb4-68d4-53b0-b47f-f37ce92da896" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of a Curve Defined Parametrically With Trigonometric Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "93911837-1abd-5695-838e-0d78fbca8630" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{x + 4}{5})^{2} + (\\frac{y + 1}{2})^{2} = 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098515} {:answer/id #uuid "af696adf-49eb-5094-96ca-2aec3c107333" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{x + 4}{5})^{2} + (\\frac{y - 1}{2})^{2} = 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098516} {:answer/id #uuid "2a4674ff-714d-5ade-b3a0-42e6ee63bd4a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{x - 1}{2})^{2} + (\\frac{y - 4}{5})^{2} = 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098517} {:answer/id #uuid "3e74b8c9-fb4a-547e-b007-c516090fccf2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{x - 1}{2})^{2} + (\\frac{y + 4}{5})^{2} = 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098518} {:answer/id #uuid "68676ebb-6352-5133-bc22-dd971fb65ce5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{x + 1}{2})^{2} + (\\frac{y - 4}{5})^{2} = 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098519}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "68676ebb-6352-5133-bc22-dd971fb65ce5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{x + 1}{2})^{2} + (\\frac{y - 4}{5})^{2} = 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098519} :answer-field/id #uuid "8cb3e85e-b511-5963-8b3f-72d966e1c836" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098514}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "20bf63df-5014-5d31-baeb-e6e95f136242" :question/math-academy-id "q-15751" :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the curve defined parametrically as \n\n$$\nx=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-1,\\,\\,\\,y=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+4,\\,t\\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's make the trigonometric functions the subject of each equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-1\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t=\\frac{x+1}{2} \\\\ y=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+4\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t=\\frac{y-4}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the Pythagorean identity ${sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t=1$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\frac{x+1}{2})}^{2}+{(\\frac{y-4}{5})}^{2}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the given curve is ${(\\frac{x+1}{2})}^{2}+{(\\frac{y-4}{5})}^{2}=1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101125 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "78dbecb4-68d4-53b0-b47f-f37ce92da896" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of a Curve Defined Parametrically With Trigonometric Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "81bb9a03-c337-5557-bf63-9b6b72dc52f9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{y}{2})^{2} - (\\frac{x}{3})^{2} = 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098521} {:answer/id #uuid "8dafe98c-e0e8-5d1a-8ace-80fcf41a6d6f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- (\\frac{y}{3})^{2} + (\\frac{x}{2})^{2} = 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098522} {:answer/id #uuid "dbaaa4e9-cb9d-55d2-88a9-f8a2c63b0d66" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x^{2} + y^{2} = 6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098523} {:answer/id #uuid "e7061204-ab95-516a-9a50-4a5fdc5a1efd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{y}{2})^{2} - (\\frac{x}{3})^{2} = 2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098524} {:answer/id #uuid "48a824f3-35a5-538a-944c-7fe7e8732431" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{y}{3})^{2} + (\\frac{x}{2})^{2} = 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098525}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "48a824f3-35a5-538a-944c-7fe7e8732431" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{y}{3})^{2} + (\\frac{x}{2})^{2} = 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098525} :answer-field/id #uuid "25da4b65-0173-565c-ba8e-416b6d42051b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098520}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0582dce7-ef1a-5cfc-9ee5-447a325b4a34" :question/math-academy-id "q-15752" :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the curve defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,t\\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's make the trigonometric functions the subject of each equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t=\\frac{x}{2} \\\\ y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t=\\frac{y}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the Pythagorean identity ${sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\frac{y}{3})}^{2}+{(\\frac{x}{2})}^{2}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the given curve is ${(\\frac{y}{3})}^{2}+{(\\frac{x}{2})}^{2}=1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186285369 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "78dbecb4-68d4-53b0-b47f-f37ce92da896" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of a Curve Defined Parametrically With Trigonometric Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c634e6c3-cda2-5dfb-a33d-c1e865c60fe1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=1-{(\\frac{x}{3})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285371} {:answer/id #uuid "8de4e91b-d865-55a8-a215-bb5ad9542c59" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=1+{(\\frac{x}{3})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285372} {:answer/id #uuid "b19e8739-b4ac-5000-9a8b-b9e06712287f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=1+{(\\frac{x}{2})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285373} {:answer/id #uuid "55c47b5a-ac8f-5c23-be2a-727e9abcdd19" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=1-3{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285374} {:answer/id #uuid "b31d0db4-2412-5c7e-a81b-24094e7b2a01" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=1+{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285375}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8de4e91b-d865-55a8-a215-bb5ad9542c59" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=1+{(\\frac{x}{3})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285372} :answer-field/id #uuid "0523683b-dd86-5d70-801b-158ffe2a1306" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285370}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7ce3b0b3-27e3-5f38-ad49-c4188b0daf7a" :question/math-academy-id "q-15761" :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the curve defined parametrically as \n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{cot}⁡t,\\,\\,\\,y=\\operatorname{csc}⁡t,\\,t\\in (0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}).\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's make the trigonometric functions the subject of each equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=3\\operatorname{cot}⁡t\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{cot}⁡t=\\frac{x}{3} \\\\ y=\\operatorname{csc}⁡t\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{csc}⁡t=y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDividing the Pythagorean identity ${sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t=1$ by ${sin}^{2}⁡t$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡t}{{sin}^{2}⁡t}+\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡t}{{sin}^{2}⁡t} & =\\frac{1}{{sin}^{2}⁡t} \\\\ 1+{(\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t})}^{2} & ={(\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t})}^{2} \\\\ 1+{cot}^{2}⁡t & ={csc}^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\operatorname{cot}⁡t=\\frac{x}{3}$ and $\\operatorname{csc}⁡t=y$ into this formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1+{(\\frac{x}{3})}^{2}={y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the given curve is ${y}^{2}=1+{(\\frac{x}{3})}^{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186285376 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "79107609-77e6-55e9-970d-da63c39daf0a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of a Curve Defined Parametrically"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05c3766d-30d2-536f-92b5-c3bb14d03783" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x^2-4x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285378} {:answer/id #uuid "1c7fc5ff-31b3-5e16-9851-ec767b6d80bf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x^2+4x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285379}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1c7fc5ff-31b3-5e16-9851-ec767b6d80bf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x^2+4x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285379} :answer-field/id #uuid "9a1ccaa9-f0ed-5847-8879-af06dd7d1c30" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285377}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fc90ad49-5a43-5603-ac83-3120ed37c3a4" :question/math-academy-id "q-320871" :question/problem "Consider the curve defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=t-2,\\,\\,\\,y={t}^{2}-4,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nThe Cartesian equation of this curve is\n\n$y=$\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation, we eliminate $t.$ First, we use the $x$ equation to solve for $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=t-2\\,⇒\\,t=x+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting $t=x+2$ into our expression for $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =(x+2)^{2}-4 \\\\ y & ={x}^{2}+4x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the Cartesian equation of the given curve is \n$y={x}^{2}+4x.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186285380 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "79107609-77e6-55e9-970d-da63c39daf0a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of a Curve Defined Parametrically"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d2b61af0-015e-5207-9580-411ca515e769" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "((3x-1)^2-4)(2-3x)^2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285382}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d2b61af0-015e-5207-9580-411ca515e769" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "((3x-1)^2-4)(2-3x)^2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285382} :answer-field/id #uuid "7a9e7a06-a7ad-5fea-bd64-541162aae1b3" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285381}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "01251fc0-f57a-5dfd-8833-fe87964205c0" :question/math-academy-id "q-321074" :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the curve defined parametrically by\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{t+1}{3},\\,\\,\\,y=({t}^{2}-4)(1-t)^{2},\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\n$y=$\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation, we eliminate $t.$ First, we use the $x$ equation to solve for $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\frac{t+1}{3}\\,⇒\\,t=3x-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting $t=3x-1$ into our expression for $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =((3x-1)^{2}-4){(1-(3x-1))}^{2} \\\\  & =(9{x}^{2}-6x+1-4)(2-3x)^{2} \\\\  & =(9{x}^{2}-6x-3)(2-3x)^{2} \\\\  & =3(3{x}^{2}-2x-1)(2-3x)^{2} \\\\  & =3(3x+1)(x-1)(2-3x)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the Cartesian equation of the given curve is $y=3(3x+1)(x-1)(2-3x)^{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186285383 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "79107609-77e6-55e9-970d-da63c39daf0a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of a Curve Defined Parametrically"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "83b629ec-0fcd-5e00-975a-de33f9ea118e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left(\\frac{1+x}{2x}\\right)^2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285385}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "83b629ec-0fcd-5e00-975a-de33f9ea118e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left(\\frac{1+x}{2x}\\right)^2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285385} :answer-field/id #uuid "88d37378-5483-53f8-8d90-61fa3cee1f14" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285384} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2686efd1-0658-53d1-ab66-14ea0dd151d1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285387}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2686efd1-0658-53d1-ab66-14ea0dd151d1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285387} :answer-field/id #uuid "eb5c5077-c40d-5cca-8f37-702494277cc0" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285386}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "67774c28-5664-5512-8ad3-78b93096bff5" :question/math-academy-id "q-321141" :question/problem "Consider the curve defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{1}{2t+1},\\,\\,\\,y=(t+1)^{2},\\,t\\ne -\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nThe Cartesian equation of this curve is\n\n$y=$\n{{field-1}}$,\\,x\\ne $\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation, we eliminate $t.$ First, we use the $x$ equation to solve for $t:$\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{1}{2t+1}\\,⇒\\,2t+1=\\frac{1}{x}\\,⇒\\,t=\\frac{1}{2x}-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, substituting $t=\\frac{1}{2x}-\\frac{1}{2}$ into our expression for $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={(\\frac{1}{2x}-\\frac{1}{2}+1)}^{2} \\\\  & ={(\\frac{1}{2x}+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} \\\\  & ={(\\frac{1+x}{2x})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(1+x)^{2}}{4{x}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the Cartesian equation of the given curve is $y=\\frac{(1+x)^{2}}{4{x}^{2}}.$\n\nFinally, since $t\\ne -\\frac{1}{2}$ and $x=\\frac{1}{2t+1}$ we must have $x\\ne 0.$\n\nTherefore, the complete Cartesian equation is\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{(1+x)^{2}}{4{x}^{2}},\\,x\\ne 0.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186285388 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "79107609-77e6-55e9-970d-da63c39daf0a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of a Curve Defined Parametrically"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ab2cdb10-b33a-5a4b-8829-e0d571cc75f7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{x-5},\\,x\\ne 5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285390} {:answer/id #uuid "f92eecc0-fe92-51ca-a4c0-c3fca69528f5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\frac{2}{x},\\,x\\ne 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285391} {:answer/id #uuid "6bcb4c4e-4b0c-51f8-896d-bbc8c716d0ce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{x+5},\\,x\\ne -5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285392} {:answer/id #uuid "befb103f-f7d7-5b97-bce7-b0c6058bf32d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}},\\,x\\ne 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285393} {:answer/id #uuid "818ee6df-487f-54dc-8aad-ac170036e72a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{5}{x},\\,x\\ne 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285394}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ab2cdb10-b33a-5a4b-8829-e0d571cc75f7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{x-5},\\,x\\ne 5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285390} :answer-field/id #uuid "a5a46568-456f-5b9d-99a2-7e660691c054" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285389}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8fbad9a1-a257-5299-b921-baa16148a38e" :question/math-academy-id "q-6828" :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the curve defined parametrically by\n\n$$\nx={t}^{2}+5,\\,\\,\\,y=\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}},\\,t\\ne 0.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation, we eliminate $t.$ First, we use the $x$ equation to solve for ${t}^{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x={t}^{2}+5\\,⇒\\,{t}^{2}=x-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting ${t}^{2}=x-5$ into our expression for $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{1}{x-5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the given curve is $y=\\frac{1}{x-5}.$\n\nFinally, since $t\\ne 0$ and $x={t}^{2}+5,$ we must have $x\\ne 5.$ \n\nSo, the complete Cartesian equation is\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{1}{x-5},\\,x\\ne 5.\n$$"}]}
